WEBVTT

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:07.240
In this short film, you'll
learn about how the brain and bladder

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:14.247
connect and work together to control
bladder emptying.

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:15.449
The bladder stores urine

00:00:15.449 --> 00:00:19.886
until you are ready to empty it

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:20.587
to urinate.

00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:28.295
The bladder send signals along the spinal
cord to your brain that it's filling up.

00:00:28.295 --> 00:00:28.862
This signals

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:32.199
the feeling that you need to urinate
when everything is working normally.

00:00:32.199 --> 00:00:37.504
You can then decide
when to empty your bladder.

00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:38.438
The bladder receives

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:41.608
signals as it fills with urine to indicate
the need to urinate.

00:00:41.608 --> 00:00:45.745
When the bladder is about halfway
full, around 250 milliliters,

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:48.882
you get the first sensation
you need to urinate.

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:51.017
As the bladder continues to fill,
the sensation

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:54.554
becomes stronger
and the urgency to urinate increases.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:59.259
The bladder muscles relax as your bladder
fills, but contract when you urinate.

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:02.195
The sphincter muscle controls
the flow of urine out of the bladder,

00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:02.863
which squeezes

00:01:02.863 --> 00:01:07.801
the urethra shut as the bladder
fills and relaxes as the bladder is empty.

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:10.537
How often you
need to urinate depends on several things,

00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:13.740
such as your fluid intake,
how much urine you produce, and how much

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.776
urine your bladder comfortably holds.

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:19.446
So that's a short explanation
of the connection between your brain

00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:23.446
and bladder and how they work together
when it comes to emptying your bladder.
